{"type":"result","subtype":"success","is_error":false,"api_error_status":null,"duration_ms":17242,"duration_api_ms":18529,"num_turns":6,"result":"I wrote `src/cpufreq/cpufreq.bt`, but I haven't run it. I also didn't check that `power:cpu_frequency` exists on this machine.\n\nThe script attaches to the `power:cpu_frequency` tracepoint. Each time a CPU changes frequency, it prints the CPU number, the new frequency in MHz, the previous frequency and the current process. When you press Ctrl-C it prints the number of changes per CPU, a histogram of frequencies per CPU, and the last frequency seen on each CPU.\n\nRun it with `sudo bpftrace src/cpufreq/cpufreq.bt`.\n\n- **Idle systems:** the tracepoint only fires on frequency changes, so an idle machine may print nothing for a while. Running some load in another terminal will trigger it.\n- **Process column:** `comm` is whatever process is on the CPU when the change happens. It is often `swapper` or a kernel thread, not the process that caused the change.\n- **First change per CPU:** the previous frequency shows 0, because there is no earlier reading.\n- **VMs and some drivers:** the tracepoint may not fire at all. Check with `sudo bpftrace -l 'tracepoint:power:*'`.","stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"d91bdb57-3b3e-4076-97ba-16561b12a8a3","total_cost_usd":0.06239005,"usage":{"input_tokens":10,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2003,"cache_read_input_tokens":102626,"output_tokens":1574,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2003},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":405,"cache_read_input_tokens":26452,"cache_creation_input_tokens":832,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":832,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001":{"inputTokens":351,"outputTokens":20,"cacheReadInputTokens":0,"cacheCreatio